<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="41022" href="schedule/2021/fall/EALC/495/41022.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2021" href="schedule/2021.xml">2021</calendarYear><term id="120218" href="schedule/2021/fall.xml">Fall 2021</term><subject id="EALC" href="schedule/2021/fall/EALC.xml">East Asian Language and Culture</subject><course id="495" href="schedule/2021/fall/EALC/495.xml">Topics in Asian Religions</course></parents><sectionNumber>A4 </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Lotus Sutra  Japanese Culture</sectionTitle><creditHours>4 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>The Lotus Sutra and Japanese Culture. Perhaps no other Buddhist scripture has had more impact on East Asian Culture than The Lotus Sutra.  After reading the sutra and recent scholarship concerning its production and reception in India and China, we will examine the fundamental role the sutra played in Japanese Buddhism, with special attention to medieval Tendai and Nichiren.  Attention will also be given to the impact the Lotus Sutra has had on art, literature, gender roles, and Japanese society.</sectionText><sectionNotes>Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign.</sectionNotes><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2021-08-23Z</startDate><endDate>2021-12-08Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>04:00 PM</start><end>06:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1126</roomNumber><buildingName>Foreign Languages Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Callahan" firstName="C">Callahan, C</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>